x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <typeinfo>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. The descriptors this target names - `??_R0H@8`, `??_R0?AUD@@@8`, `??_R0PEBD@8` - and the `__RTtypeid` call are measured in tests/out-measure/tid.clang.s on 2026-09-26 and agree; typeid.cpp, which declares std::type_info itself, is compared here on the Itanium targets.
arm64-darwin  a difference about emission and about the library, not about names: cpp11 emits <typeinfo>'s inline members as the functions the header defines them as, where libc++'s carry its ABI tag (`nameB9nqe220106`) and reach a non-unique-RTTI helper of its own; and clang calls `___cxa_bad_typeid` for `typeid(*pb)` should pb be null, which cpp11 does not test for. Every name both compilers emit agrees.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes a C++ standard library header, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What this target emits is checked on the Windows box instead.
